Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used  
• Musical_Preferences.StyleID : join key that tells us which style a customer prefers  
• Musical_Preferences.PreferenceSeq : the ranking of that preference (1 = first, 2 = second, 3 = third) – will drive the “1st / 2nd / 3rd” counts  
• Musical_Styles.StyleID : join key back to Musical_Preferences  
• Musical_Styles.StyleName : the friendly description we want to display in the result set  
Condition that will be useful  
• Musical_Preferences.PreferenceSeq IN (1,2,3) – we only care about the first three preferences  

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (each capped with LIMIT 20) leading up to the final answer.  
(The comments above each query explain what the query shows.)

```sql
-- 1) Distinct preference‑sequence values that exist
SELECT DISTINCT "PreferenceSeq"
FROM "Musical_Preferences"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List a few rows of raw preferences data
SELECT *
FROM "Musical_Preferences"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Show all musical styles (reference table)
SELECT *
FROM "Musical_Styles"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Join preferences to style names (no filtering yet)
SELECT p."CustomerID",
       s."StyleName",
       p."PreferenceSeq"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Only the first‑three preferences with their style names
SELECT s."StyleName", p."PreferenceSeq"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
WHERE  p."PreferenceSeq" IN (1,2,3)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Count total occurrences of each style (any ranking)
SELECT s."StyleName",
       COUNT(*) AS "Total_Picks"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
GROUP  BY s."StyleName"
ORDER  BY "Total_Picks" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count how many times each style is the 1st preference
SELECT s."StyleName",
       COUNT(*) AS "First_Place_Count"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
WHERE  p."PreferenceSeq" = 1
GROUP  BY s."StyleName"
ORDER  BY "First_Place_Count" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Conditional aggregation: 1st, 2nd, and 3rd counts side‑by‑side
SELECT s."StyleName",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Firsts",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Seconds",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 3 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Thirds"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
GROUP  BY s."StyleName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Same as #8 but restricted to sequences 1‑3 only (defensive)
SELECT s."StyleName",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Firsts",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Seconds",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 3 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Thirds"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
WHERE  p."PreferenceSeq" IN (1,2,3)
GROUP  BY s."StyleName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final answer: every musical style with its # of 1st, 2nd, 3rd picks
SELECT s."StyleName",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Pref_1_Count",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Pref_2_Count",
       SUM(CASE WHEN p."PreferenceSeq" = 3 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "Pref_3_Count"
FROM   "Musical_Preferences" AS p
JOIN   "Musical_Styles"      AS s  ON s."StyleID" = p."StyleID"
WHERE  p."PreferenceSeq" IN (1,2,3)
GROUP  BY s."StyleName"
ORDER  BY s."StyleName"
LIMIT 20;
```
